A||N||29||colts60||how are 2m4 fieros in snow||||6||
Z||000000||colts60||11-01-2000||07:02 PM||||i'm 16 and i baught a fiero for my first car and i'm wondering how they go in snow being that i live Penn. do they go or should i put it in a garage for the winter or try to drive it||||reg||6
Z||000001||2xsess||11-01-2000||07:06 PM||||I'd say that since it is your first car that would lead me to beleive that you don't have must wheel time. So it wouldn't be a good idea to learn to drive in snow in your Fiero.<BR>I lived in northeast PA during the two blizzards (1993 & 1995) I drove a Fiero through both of them, after the state opened the roads up again. It's not a 4x4 but if you put snow tires on and take it easy it'll get you where you need to be.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||11-01-2000||07:41 PM||||Im from pa too, the fiero is a lot better then; list: RWD/2WD full size truck, RWD/2WD small truck, Ford Mustang, Chevy Camaro, and believe it or not Most FWD cars....<P>a lot depends on your tires though, the skinnier, the better.  if you put 225/60s on it youll slide around a lot but it will get up hills easily, if you have 185/70s or something like that, it shouldnt slide much but it might not make it up those big PA hills without spinning||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||11-01-2000||08:17 PM||||What the others said. If you get on the throttle too much though you will spin out easily. If you feather the throttle and go slow the fiero is better than most rear wheel drive carts are in the snow.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||overlord||11-01-2000||08:56 PM||||the first winter i had my 2m4, i chickend out and parked it.<P>last winter i needed it, and it wasnt  tooo bad. It is about the same as my cousning trans-am on snow. I only hit the ditch once!<P>the only probelms i  had with snow was my brakes freezing up, when breaking trail through 4-5 inches of snow on gravel roads....yes i live in the boonies.<P>You just have to drive a lot more carefullly with a fiero in snow.  You may find yourself spinning in circles if you have a heavy  foot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BHoldwick||11-01-2000||11:34 PM||||Don't know but I'm taking mine into Colorado this winter. Good luck with yours. Ft Carson here I come!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||robertjay||11-01-2000||11:41 PM||||Put a set of studded tires on and mine would tow 2 snowmobiles up stevens pass in WA state.  I got some funny looks passing full size Fords, but never got in a wreck.  (Moral) With the correct tires for conditions, these little things will climb trees with the right touch!  It's all in the right foot.<p>[This message has been edited by robertjay (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||11-02-2000||12:53 AM||||Drove my 2m4 all last winter.  Only spun out twice... once because I got on the throttle too hard at the end of a cloverleaf, and the other.. well, i'm not sure why.  It was really windy and the car just kept getting pushed around.. oh yeah, i hit some snow that was piled up on the shoulder.  Definatly get some skinnier tires, I have 185/80 13 on the 2m4.  After I got my GT I had some trouble where there was just some sand on the road, and that was with 205's.. now with 225's it gets a bit squirlley(sp) even in a light rain... <P>I found it easier to just stay on the throttle and drift through most turns, after some practice it gets real easy! anyways, I like taking a right hand turn with the wheel turned left  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  and definatly find a big (and empty!) parking lot after there's some snow on the ground and practice!<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shiner||11-02-2000||01:30 AM||||What are Fieros like to drive in the snow?  A whole lot of fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||11-02-2000||02:08 AM||||BHoldwick,<P>When are you coming to Colorado?  So far we haven't had any "real" snow, but that'll change soon enough.  <P>I'm hoping for another mild winter (like last year), but somehow I think we're going to get socked!<P>LMK when you get here or if you need  anything for your Fiero.  I have a friend stationed at Ft. Carson.....he owns an '86 GT.  I'm sure you'll see him around base.<P>   ||||reg||10
Z||000010||ChadMan||11-02-2000||08:45 AM||||Standard is right - tires make all the difference with Fieros and snow.  Here's my experience:<P>84 Coupe + 185/85R13 Dunlop Axioms = great snow car<P>86 SE V6 + 215/60R14 Firehawk SS = worse snow car, still not too bad<P>88 Coupe + 215/60R14 Yokohama S305 = terrible snow car - don't stop going uphill, you'll never get started again||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||11-02-2000||09:20 AM||||Friends,<P>I have had my 1987 EC with the stock 195.75R13's (cheap $25 tires) for the last few winters.  It is an automatic, so I do not have as much control over the rear wheels.  During the many trips to and from Flint, MI to Cincinnati, OH I have only had one instance of severe loss of traction and I was not driving as I should have.  It occured on I-75 at one of the many overpasses where the road surface changes abruptly... I was excessively speeding at the time with about 2" of fresh powder on the unscraped road.  <P>My Fiero is as good as my 86 J-body was, is better than any of the newer cars I have driven during snow storms (89, 92 & 96 W bodies, 97 Taurus, 99 Sebring, 96 Transport...).  Remember that mine is Duke powered.  The lack of power coupled with thin tires make this vehicle as good as anything I have driven in the snow.  It is VERY predictable and VERY driveable.  The only difference between maneuvering this vehicle is how much momentum builds up when the rear end swings out since the rear weight bias.<P>Any suspension flaw will decrease winter driveability.  Perhaps this is the problem with many of the Fiero's out there... high miles and seriously sub-par suspensions.  I drive approximately 30K miles per year in this vehicle so it is not that I do not have much experience driving in the white stuff. <P>My best reccomendations would be to learn the limits of your vehicle BEFORE hitting public roads.  It doesn't matter what you are driving.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||2
Z||000012||jlhuber||11-02-2000||09:50 AM||||I am originally from Michigan, and as everybody who has ever driven in snow knows and says, "It is all in the tires and driving style."  If you are a lead-footed, hot-roddin', race driver wannabe, you will never stay on the road in winter.  For tires, it is the same concept as 4-wheelin', tall tires cut through the mud and wide tires just float on the top and spin.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Though in that case it also depends on tread pattern too, 'swamper boggers will gig you through it while any street tread will try to float over it it.  I had 205/50/15 & 215/50/15 BFG Radial T/A's on my 86 gt, BFG... my brand of chioce.  That was the car that I felt most comfortable driving in the winter.  I mean, I hated to be driving it because of the salt, but I felt safe doing it.  Anyways, enough of my senseless rambling, just get the right tires and drive responsibly (sp?).  ||||reg||4
Z||000013||Jaygee79||11-02-2000||10:19 AM||||I haven't really had any trouble with mine.  Only problem is if you hit the brakes or the gas too hard, just like any other car.  I just drive kind of slow and take it easy.  No problems.  Drives better than some other newer cars I've been in.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 11-02-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000014||Fiero5||11-02-2000||11:17 AM||||Having driven a wide variety of Fieros in the winters of New England for years, I can say with experience that they handle as well or better than any other regular car or truck on the roads.<P>I have used both narrow and wide (with deep tread) tires, and found the Fiero to handle and respond very well to differing winter road conditions. I have even driven a Fiero through deep unplowed snow, where the snow was blowing up and over the hood. In deep snow, try not to  stop if you can.<P>Keep in mind that no matter what you drive, black ice will get you every time.<P>So, when winter weather has arrived, jump into Fiero with confidence.<P>Steve <A HREF="http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Formula||11-02-2000||01:43 PM||||has anyone used 205-60 Front and 225-60 Rear in the snow?  this will be my first winter with this tire setup||||reg||1
Z||000016||btoth||11-02-2000||01:54 PM||||I've got BFGoodrich Comp T/A VR4's on my '86 GT... sweet tires!  (Stock 205/60/15s and 215/60/15s in the back.)  Very good grip for wide tires but I can still break them loose if I want (which I usually do)...  I like driving sideways down the street.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>I've also never had my car get stuck, not even when I slid on black ice into someone's lawn!! Haha.  But my parents FWD drive cars get stuck all the time in deep snow... go fig.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 11-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||11-02-2000||02:18 PM||||Tires are the first thing to get right. As for handling, I have driven thru many snow storms in a Fiero. Once from New York to Chicago in January. I think fieros handle great in snow, As long as you take it easy. One snow storm I was able to drive to and from work without any problems but a friend with a Firebird got stuck often. With the engine closer to the middle of the car your rear wheel traction is much better. In very deep snow turning slower is better. I have always used BFGoodrich tires. Never got stuck. ||||reg||14
Z||000018||colts60||11-02-2000||06:25 PM||||i'm glad their not that bad i guss i baught the right car<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Haze_Performance||11-02-2000||07:12 PM||||I picked up my 2M4 in Feb. 2000 (SNOW SNOW SNOW!!!) I didnt have any problems.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">   <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT_4.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000020||HAGS||11-03-2000||03:12 AM||||I am 18 and my first and hopefully lifetime car is also a Fiero 2m4...I dont know if its just my car but it handles terribly, and all the books I have about it say the fiero is supposed to have great handling characteristics...<P>I bought the car for $800 for the Ford Dealer my mom works for...it broke down daily until I replaced the engine mount and dog bone...waiting for the transmission mount now.  Back to the subject...my car is terrible in rain, im guessing because of the high lift and low weight...luckily I live in Texas and the last time it snowed in San Antonio the Fieros were just babies.   I have never driven in snow, but if its anything like mud then I wouldnt dare go out without leaving a suicide note on the fridge.||||reg||13
Z||000021||jlhuber||11-03-2000||08:25 AM||||Just something that I have noticed over the years.  People who have never driven in snow or heavy rain usually do not know how to react the frist few times they encounter it.  Case in point, the guy I work with is from Atlanta, GA.  Winter of '99 was the first snow (so he says) that he had ever driven in.  There was approx. 3" of snow on the ground, and he was too chicken to drive in it for the fear of he would slide off the road.  He also says that they close the public schools if there is 6inches or more snow standing on the ground.  In Michigan where I am from, it takes 3feet or more to close public schools.  People who have lived in the south all their life and then move to colder climate are generally the worse drivers who also say that their car handles like crap in the snow.  Same principle, if you have never ice skated (figure or hockey) chances have it that the first time you hit the ice with your skates, you will not do so great till you figure it out.  If you have ever driven other cars (read: fwd/rwd/2wd trucks) you will find that the fiero will handle better than most other cars.  Maintenence of the vehicle is also a factor.  If you have bad ball joints, bent rims, or something like that, chances have it you may run into problems in the snow.  Course something like that you will have problems on dry roads to, but it was for the sake of the arguement. Man, I gotta quit these random outloud thoughts... where's my beer!?||||reg||4
Z||000022||mrfiero||11-03-2000||12:39 PM||||You are right sir!!  I have lived in Colorado for most of the past 16 years and can say I am a good "snow driver".  I moved to Alabama 2 years ago to work at the Fiero Factory and during one storm that winter Huntsville got a little snow and a lot of ice.  The people had no idea what to do!  There were many mulit-car pile ups because they were following too close, going too fast and generally not staying on the road.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>Much of the problem was due to the fact that these conditions rarely, if ever happen down there and the people are just unprepared.  I mean, they don't have the equipment to clear the roads (plows, etc.), so when this happens it effectively shuts things down.  That storm had the entire city (town?) shut down.  That same storm here would have just been a nuisance.   <BR>||||reg||10
Z||000023||Fiero5||11-04-2000||12:09 AM||||The two key elements are 1) How you drive based on road conditions and 2) what type of tires/tread you have.<P>If the road is slippery and you are bombing right a long, this is what could happen.<BR>(No cows were hurt in doing this demonstration)<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||fierospeeder||11-04-2000||01:48 AM||||one thing to remember is its easy to lock up the brakes while driving.  Try to take it easy on those cold wet nights when its slippery.  Also, bad tires will make you spin out on wet or icy roads when you spin the rear tires, and you find yourself doing a 360.||||reg||1
Z||000025||StuGood||11-04-2000||01:08 PM||||'Snow problem...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I probably tend to drive my Fieros more cautiously than other cars, just because I don't want anything to happen to them!  Other than that, I'd say Fieros do just fine; yes, they're actually fun on the snow! <P>Specifics? What everybody said earlier.  Skinny tires help.  Wimpy engine helps.  Practice helps (in empty parking lots and/or abandoned roads, etc.).  Patience and care, and alertness and courtesy help most of all.  Plus, I find that courtesy is usually highly contagious - a very good thing    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>Black ice is bad news - watch it, especially when you are approaching an overpass.  Start slowing down early when you approach an overpass, because if there's ice, it'll probably be too late to slow down when you're on it.<P>Since you're from Pennsylvania, you'll get your share of practice driving in the snow!  That'll put you ahead of those, who've never seen snows    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !    <P>BTW, with a name like "colts60," I take it you're a Colts fan?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000026||KnightRyder31||11-04-2000||04:06 PM||||One word: Interesting.||||reg||1
Z||000027||KnightRyder31||11-04-2000||04:11 PM||||And to add on that.......if you are a good, responsible driver, you should do fine.<BR>I had to learn how to drive in the snow with a 76' Trans Am 455 4-speed, that was fun. <BR>Oh, since your sixteen, you might like to race around...well try not to in snow. If you do, don't make any sudden turns(or any movement for that matter) going very fast.<BR>Oh, and good luck, I'm sure you will do fine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||colts60||11-04-2000||07:20 PM||||not a colts fan<BR>my high school team is marian colts<BR>9-1 <BR>1990 state champians||||reg||1
Z||000029||Necromancer||11-05-2000||11:58 AM||||My 8 1/2 cents from Upstate New York...<P>I liked driving my '85 coupe in the snow, it was usually very nimble and easily drove around other cars you'd think would do better, BUT - You have to pay attention to what you are doing (driving), its very easy to 'lose it' if your mind is somewhere else.<P><BR>||||reg||1
